    {
      "item": "Resource: The specific subject within a sub-category. For example, if an admin adds a new admin, then the resource is the name of the admin that was added.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          8
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "Admin ID: The admin's login ID or the internal API user (oauth-<rolename>$@<orgid>.<cloud-domain>) if an APIs action was authenticated by an external OAuth 2.0 authentication server.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          8
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "Client IP: The source IP address for the admin or the client application's IP address that executed the API.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          9
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "Interface: The means by which the action was performed.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          9
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "The interface values are: Admin UI or API.",
      "depth": 2,
      "strong": [
        [
          26,
          34
        ],
        [
          38,
          41
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "Trace ID: The trace ID is generated and logged for transactions associated with Internet & SaaS API requests made via Zscaler OneAPI. The trace ID helps admins correlate API transactions with the OneAPI platform and you can use the trace ID for debugging purposes.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          8
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "Result: The outcome of an action.",
      "depth": 1,
      "strong": [
        [
          0,
          6
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "item": "If the action was a success, a green circle with a checkmark inside is displayed.",
      "depth": 2
    },
    {
      "item": "If the action was a failure, a red circle with an X inside is displayed.",
      "depth": 2
    },
    {
      "item": "Policy rule changes that occur incidentally as a result of admin or API client actions on other rules are not shown in audit logs. For example, when a new rule is created in a higher order (e.g., order 1), the rule order for all existing rules shifts down (i.e., an existing rule with order 1 changes to 2 and so on). In this scenario, only the new rule creation is displayed and the incidental rule order changes to other rules are not shown in audit logs. However, when you use the API to retrieve the list of policy rules, such as Firewall Filtering rules, the API response reflects all changes to rules, including incidental changes. To learn more, see the API Reference.",
      "depth": 0
    },
    {
      "item": "See configuration changes.",
      "depth": 0,
      "number": 6
    },
    {
      "item": "Click the configuration changes you want to view. You can view differences between the pre-configuration and post-configuration changes.",
      "depth": 0
    },
    {
      "item": "There are two types of changes you can view:",
      "depth": 0
    },
    {
